GitLab Runner: smallest resource-only configuration
===================================================

The checked ConfigMap named gitlab-runner-config is generated by the
GitLab Runner Operator. Do not add the lines to that generated ConfigMap
because the Operator can overwrite them.

There are two small changes:

1. Create a new ConfigMap containing the resource lines.
2. Add its name to spec.config in the existing Runner resource.


Change 1: create the resource ConfigMap
=======================================

In OpenShift, create this YAML:

---
apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: gitlab-runner-job-resources
  namespace: gitlab-system
data:
  config.toml: |
    [[runners]]
      [runners.kubernetes]
        cpu_request = "1"
        cpu_limit = "4"
        memory_request = "2Gi"
        memory_limit = "6Gi"
---

Apply it:

oc apply -f gitlab-runner-job-resources.yaml


Change 2: update the existing Runner
====================================

Open:

OpenShift -> Installed Operators -> GitLab Runner -> gitlab-runner -> YAML

Find the existing spec section:

spec:
  buildImage: alpine
  ca: gitlab-cert
  gitlabUrl: 'https://gitlab.apps.asmonpeclr.np.asmo.com'
  token: gitlab-runner-secret

Add this one line under spec:

  config: gitlab-runner-job-resources

The final spec must look like this:

spec:
  buildImage: alpine
  ca: gitlab-cert
  config: gitlab-runner-job-resources
  gitlabUrl: 'https://gitlab.apps.asmonpeclr.np.asmo.com'
  token: gitlab-runner-secret

Click Save.

Alternatively, run:

oc patch runner gitlab-runner -n gitlab-system --type merge \
  -p '{"spec":{"config":"gitlab-runner-job-resources"}}'


Verify
======

oc get runner gitlab-runner -n gitlab-system \
  -o jsonpath='{.spec.config}{"\n"}'

Expected:

gitlab-runner-job-resources

Run a new pipeline and inspect its job pod:

oc get pods -n gitlab-system

oc describe pod <JOB-POD-NAME> -n gitlab-system

The build container should show:

Requests:
  cpu: 1
  memory: 2Gi

Limits:
  cpu: 4
  memory: 6Gi


Important
=========

This is the smallest configuration for build pod CPU and memory only.
It does not change the Runner manager pod, concurrency, cache, storage,
job timeout, or GitLab artifact upload limit.


GitLab Container Registry
=========================

The pipeline will push the container image to:

$CI_REGISTRY_IMAGE:$CI_COMMIT_SHORT_SHA

For this project, GitLab automatically supplies CI_REGISTRY_IMAGE with the
correct Container Registry repository path.

Go to:

GitLab project -> Settings -> CI/CD -> Variables

Add these two variables:

Key:
GITLAB_REGISTRY_USERNAME

Value:
devops

Key:
GITLAB_REGISTRY_PASSWORD

Value:
<DEVOPS USER PASSWORD OR ACCESS TOKEN>

For both variables:

- Select Masked.
- Select Protected only if pipelines run only on protected branches.
- Do not add these values to .gitlab-ci.yml.

The devops user or access token must have these registry permissions:

read_registry
write_registry

The image job uses Kaniko on the OCP Runner. It does not require Docker,
Docker-in-Docker, or a privileged Runner.

It consumes the JAR artifact from build_maven and pushes two image tags:

$CI_REGISTRY_IMAGE:$CI_COMMIT_SHORT_SHA
$CI_REGISTRY_IMAGE:$CI_COMMIT_REF_SLUG

The pipeline reads the credentials from:

$GITLAB_REGISTRY_USERNAME
$GITLAB_REGISTRY_PASSWORD

The credentials are written only to Kaniko's temporary Docker configuration
inside the CI job pod. They are not stored in the repository.

Because the internal GitLab Registry may use a private certificate, the
current Kaniko command includes:

--skip-tls-verify-registry "$CI_REGISTRY"

Remove this option after the GitLab Registry CA certificate is trusted by the
Runner.

After the pipeline succeeds, open:

GitLab project -> Deploy -> Container Registry

The image should have a tag matching the pipeline commit short SHA.

If the Container Registry page is unavailable, the GitLab administrator must
enable and configure GitLab Container Registry before this job can push.
